    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Sundiata Keita defeated King Soumaoro Kanté of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sosso-empire">Sosso</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kirina">Battle of Kirina</a>, c. 1235, and became the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">Mansa</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a>.</li><li>Most of what is told of his life comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-sundiata">Epic of Sundiata</a>, an oral tradition of the Maninka <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griots">griots</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a>, writing a century later, confirm that he existed.</li><li>The social and political norms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, the Gbara and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kouroukan-fouga">Kouroukan Fouga</a>, were first being codified in his reign, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manding">Manden</a> Charter is listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> as intangible cultural heritage.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The name Sundiata praises him through his mother: it means &quot;the lion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sogolon-conde">Sogolon</a>&quot;, from Sogolon and Jata, lion.</p></aside>
    </section>

