    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1217</span><span class="rf-text">Sundiata is born, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghan-kon-fatta">Naré Maghann Konaté</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sogolon-conde">Sogolon Condé</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1235</span><span class="rf-text">Sundiata and his allies defeat King Soumaoro Kanté of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sosso-empire">Sosso</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kirina">Battle of Kirina</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1235</span><span class="rf-text">He becomes the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">Mansa</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> and sets his capital at Niani</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">13th century</span><span class="rf-text">His general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiramakhan-traore">Tiramakhan Traore</a> defeats the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolof-empire">Jolof</a> and conquers the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegambia">Senegambia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaabu-empire">Kaabu</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1255</span><span class="rf-text">Sundiata dies, by the generally accepted account drowned in the Sankarani River</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sundiata Keita was born around 1217. According to the oral traditions passed down by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griots">griots</a> (djeli or jeliw), he was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghan-kon-fatta">Naré Maghann Konaté</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a> king, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sogolon-conde">Sogolon Condé</a>, called the daughter of the "buffalo woman". The tradition tells that Sundiata was crippled from childhood and that his mother was ridiculed by her co-wives for her son's disability. Determined to walk like his peers, he one day got up and walked, the epic says miraculously, and became a leader among them.</p><p>His paternal half-brother, Dankaran Touman, and Dankaran's mother, Sassouma Bereté, are remembered as cruel and resentful of Sundiata and his mother, and their cruelty grew after the death of Naré Maghann. To escape threats on her son's life, Sogolon took her children into exile, which by the tradition lasted many years and led through lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mema">Mema</a>, whose king gave them asylum and admired Sundiata for his courage, granting him a senior position. The Mandinka epic gives no dates; the dates and a genealogy come from Arab and North African writers who visited the region about a century later.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>When King Soumaoro Kanté of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sosso-empire">Sosso</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a>, messengers, by the tradition, went to look for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sogolon-conde">Sogolon</a> and her children, since a prophecy held that Sundiata would be a great leader. They found him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mema">Mema</a> and persuaded him to return and free the Mandinka. He came back with an army given to him by the king of Mema. On the plain of Siby he and the warlords of his age group, among them Tabon Wana, Kamadia Kamara, Faony Condé, Siara Kuman Konaté and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiramakhan-traore">Tiramakhan Traore</a>, formed a pact of brotherhood against the Sosso king.</p><p>The consensus is that in c. 1235 Sundiata, who had survived one of Soumaoro's earlier raids, went to war with his allies and defeated Soumaoro at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kirina">Battle of Kirina</a>. He became the first emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> and the first of the Mandinka line of kings to take the royal title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">Mansa</a>, king or emperor in Mandinka. The French ethnographer Maurice Delafosse had proposed a fuller story of the Sosso kings, including a father named Diara Kanté, but his account has been refuted by scholars such as Monteil and Cornevin, and no Diara Kanté appears in the oral sources.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kirina">Kirina</a>, Sundiata took control of the states the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sosso-empire">Sosso</a> had conquered and gave privileges to those who had fought against Soumaoro. He set his capital at Niani, near the present-day border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>. With his generals, of whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiramakhan-traore">Tiramakhan Traore</a> was among the most prominent, he went on to conquer other states, including the lands of the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a>; Sundiata himself is credited with the conquest of Diafunu and Kita. Tiramakhan defeated the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolof-empire">Jolof</a> and made his kingdom a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, then went deep into present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea-bissau">Guinea-Bissau</a>; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaabu-empire">Kaabu</a> he defeated and killed the last great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bainuk">Bainuk</a> king, Kikikor, and annexed his state.</p><p>Sundiata was not an absolute monarch, whatever his title implies. The empire was reportedly run like a federation, each clan, first the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a> clans of Traore, Kamara, Koroma, Konde and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keita-dynasty">Keita</a>, having a chief representative at court, and the Great Gbara Assembly checked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">Mansa</a>'s power, enforced his edicts and chose his successor. He controlled the region's trade routes and gold fields, and is reported to have developed agriculture and introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and weaving in Mali.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Sundiata's great enemy was Soumaoro Kanté, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sosso-empire">Sosso</a>, whom the traditions remember as a valiant warrior and as one of the true champions of the traditional African religion; according to Fyle, Soumaoro invented the balafon and the dan, a four-string guitar of hunters and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griots">griots</a>. Soumaoro's allies were defeated after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kirina">Kirina</a>, in particular the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolof-empire">Jolof</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> oral tradition tells of "the robbery of the horses": when Sundiata sent men with gold to buy horses in Jolof, the king took both gold and horses, and Sundiata sent his general to kill him. Djibril Tamsir Niane wrote that the Jolof king sided with Soumaoro because, like him, he was hostile to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><p>Sundiata's own religion is disputed. Niane alludes to his being a Muslim, but according to Fage nothing in the original epic supports this; he is remembered as a great hunter and magician whose subjects, like him, mostly held traditional beliefs. Some Muslim griots later added that he descended from companions of Muhammad and was a successor of Dhu al-Qarnayn, claims that G. Wesley Johnson calls a search for "Islamic legitimacy".</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The generally accepted year of Sundiata's death is c. 1255, but little is known of how he died, and the versions, mostly modern, differ. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a> tradition also forbids revealing the burial places of its great kings. According to some he drowned while trying to cross the Sankarani River near Niani. Delafosse wrote that he was "accidentally killed by an arrow during a ceremony", and others have held that he was assassinated at a public demonstration known as a Gitten.</p><p>The account now generally accepted is drowning in the Sankarani River near his capital, where a shrine bearing his name, Sundiata-dun, "Sundiata's deep water", still stands. His three sons, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">Mansa</a> Wali <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keita-dynasty">Keita</a>, Mansa Ouati Keita and Mansa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalifa">Khalifa</a> Keita, succeeded him as Mansas of the empire, and the famously ostentatious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a> was later to rule as his great-nephew. Towards the end of his reign, "absolute security" is reported to have "prevailed throughout his dominion". The empire he founded flourished from the 13th to the late 14th century, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> states threw off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>'s rule, some of them founding empires of their own.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Under Sundiata <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> first began to become an economic power, a trend his successors continued; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa-musa">Mansa Musa</a>, the famously wealthy ruler of the next century, was his great-nephew. Credit for Mali's conquests is shared with his generals, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiramakhan-traore">Tiramakhan Traore</a> above all, but he is remembered as a leader who commanded the loyalty of his generals and army. The Gbara and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kouroukan-fouga">Kouroukan Fouga</a>, the social and political constitution first codified in his reign, were never written down at the time and changed in the retelling, yet many of their laws have been incorporated into the constitution of modern Mali, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> lists the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manding">Manden</a> Charter as intangible cultural heritage.</p><p>He lives above all in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-sundiata">Epic of Sundiata</a>, the semi-historical, legendary poem of the Malinké that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griots">griots</a> have handed down for generations, which shows the influence of what Ralph Austen calls "Islamicate" culture. Scholars disagree even over his surname: some propose that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keita-dynasty">Keita</a> is a royal or clan name meaning heir, and that his family name was Konaté. The epic is taught far beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>; Mary Ellen Snodgrass and others have noted its likeness to Disney's The Lion King, which Disney says drew on Hamlet.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/epic-of-sundiata">Epic of Sundiata<span>c. 1255</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Sundiata Keita defeated Soumaoro Kanté of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sosso-empire">Sosso</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kirina">Kirina</a> around 1235 and became the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">Mansa</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a>, ruling from Niani with a council of clans and generals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiramakhan-traore">Tiramakhan Traore</a>. Known chiefly through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griots">griots</a>' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-sundiata">Epic of Sundiata</a>, he died around 1255, by the accepted account drowned in the Sankarani River.</em></p>

